```

In the vulnerable code snippet above, the `equals()` method is used to compare the entered password with the hardcoded "secret" string. This is vulnerable because it's case-sensitive and doesn't protect against timing attacks. An attacker could use this information to infer whether their guess was correct or not based on the time it takes for the response.

**Fixed Code Snippet:**

```java
import java.util.Scanner;
import java.security.MessageDigest;

public class SecureCode {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.println("Enter your password:");
        String password = scanner.nextLine();

        // Hash the entered password using SHA-256
        MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256");
        byte[] hashedPassword = md.digest(password.getBytes());

        // Compare the hashed password with the hashed version of the correct password
        String correctPasswordHash = "6e7996181b2c882b11708b2e37b93838e04e158a73b02964a6f3f43b24996bd6"; // Replace this with the actual hash of "secret"
        if (MessageDigest.isEqual(hashedPassword, correctPasswordHash.getBytes())) {
            System.out.println("Access granted!");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Access denied!");
        }
    }
}
```

In the fixed code snippet, the entered password is hashed using the SHA-256 algorithm before comparing it with the hashed version of the correct password. This makes the comparison case-insensitive and protects against timing attacks. Additionally, the correct password hash is stored as a constant string instead of being hardcoded directly into the if condition, which is a more secure practice.

